export default {"builtAt":"2026-10-27T18:43:28.556Z","files":{"01-the-bet/diagnostic.md":"# Three-Axis Vulnerability Diagnostic\n\n> Week 1 · Oct 26, 2026 · Draft for partner challenge\n\n## Product\n\n**Product:** Leave Ledger. California leave of absence management for employers with 5 to 200 employees. It works out which state and federal leaves an employee qualifies for, what the state pays, and the notices and deadlines the employer owes, then gives HR, the employee and the manager each their own view.\n**Your Role:** Founder and product lead (solo build, test site on Netlify with 10 test accounts).\n\n**Scoring convention used here:** Contextual Moat and Data Advantage, 5 = strongest position. Platform Exposure, 5 = most exposed.\n\n---\n\n## The market in numbers\n\n| Segment | California businesses | Why it matters |\n|---|---|---|\n| 5 to 49 employees | 328,201 | Owe CFRA, PDL, bereavement and reproductive loss leave, but not federal FMLA. Most leave tools are built around FMLA. |\n| 50 to 249 employees | 48,240 | Owe all of the above plus FMLA. Where mid-market vendors start. |\n| **5 to 249 total** | **376,441** | Rough employee base: about 9.8M (midpoint estimate per size band) |\n\nSource: EDD size-of-business table, third quarter (statewide row). The employee figure multiplies each band's count by its midpoint, so treat it as an order of magnitude, not a fact.\n\n**Price anchor:** Tilt lists $4 to $6 per employee per month (G2, Dec 2025). At that price, 9.8M employees is a $470M to $700M a year ceiling. Real reachable revenue is a small fraction of that; M3 works the actual number.\n\n---\n\n## Scores\n\n### Contextual Moat · 2/5\n*Workflow depth × switching cost. Would users leave in a weekend if a competitor showed up?*\n\n**Score rationale:** The workflow is real: a deadline clock that starts when a manager hears about a leave, stacked entitlements (PDL then CFRA bonding, with SDI and PFL pay), notices, and three role views with server-side privacy. But today Leave Ledger holds no history and no documents, and it does not touch payroll. A user could move in a weekend. Switching cost appears once it holds the 12-month rolling CFRA history, signed notices and certifications for every employee, because that record is what an employer needs in a dispute.\n\n**Named attacker (draft, for partner challenge):** **Tilt** (formerly Sparrow). $4 to $6 per employee per month, two-way Gusto sync, all leave types nationwide. If Tilt adds a small-business tier, it arrives with payroll integration on day one, which Leave Ledger lacks.\n\n---\n\n### Data Advantage · 1/5\n*Proprietary signal that compounds with usage. What do you see that OpenAI doesn't?*\n\n**Score rationale:** No users yet, so no data. The rules engine encodes public law that anyone can read. The signal that could compound:\n1. How employees and managers actually describe a need for time off (\"my mom's surgery is next month\"), mapped to the leave type HR confirmed. This trains intake.\n2. Outcomes per California leave pattern: real durations by reason, how often PDL runs into bonding, how often certifications come back late.\n3. California edge cases HR had to override, which become rules or tests.\n\nNone of this exists until roughly 50 active employers are using it.\n\n**Named attacker (draft, for partner challenge):** **ChatGPT and other general assistants.** An office manager can already ask \"my employee is pregnant, what leave do they get in California?\" and get a decent answer for free. What the assistant lacks is the employee's hours, tenure, prior leave and the deadline clock. That is the gap to own.\n\n---\n\n### Platform Exposure · 4/5\n*Encroachment risk × pivot speed. If Apple/Google/OpenAI ships your hero feature native, then what?*\n\n**Score rationale:** For this product, the platforms are the payroll and HR systems small businesses already pay for. Encroachment is already happening, one tier up:\n- **Paylocity** acquired Aidora, an AI-native leave management company, on July 9, 2026. Aimed at mid-market and enterprise.\n- **Mitratech** launched Mitratech Leave, AI-native leave compliance, at SHRM26 in June 2026. Aimed at mid-sized and multi-state employers.\n- **Gusto, Rippling and Justworks** are where California small businesses run payroll. Any of them can bundle leave tracking at no extra price.\n\nPivot speed is the one strength: the codebase is small and could become an add-on inside a payroll marketplace within weeks.\n\n**Named attacker (draft, for partner challenge):** **Gusto.** It already holds the hours, hire dates and pay that eligibility depends on. A California leave module bundled into its plans would remove the reason to buy a separate tool.\n\n---\n\n## Top Vulnerability\n\nDistribution. Small businesses buy HR software through their payroll provider. If Gusto or Rippling ships California-specific leave (CFRA and PDL stacking plus a notice clock) before Leave Ledger has paying users, a standalone product has no way in.\n\n## Confidence Level\n\n**L (low).** The product logic is sound and the gap for 5 to 49 employee employers is real on paper, but nobody outside this build has used it or said they would pay. Moves to M after 10 interviews with California office managers or HR generalists.\n","01-the-bet/prototype.md":"# The Prototype Bet\n\n> Week 1 · Oct 26, 2026\n\n## What I Built\n\nA working test site where an HR admin, a manager and an employee each see a California leave from their side. The HR admin gets stacked entitlements, a state pay estimate, the notices owed, and a deadline clock. The employee gets their balances and next steps. The manager gets dates only.\n\n## Tool Used\n\nClaude (product, rules research, code), GitHub (`francis4531/leave-ledger`), Netlify (hosting, serverless API, Blobs storage). Cursor for quick local edits from here on.\n\n## Prototype Link\n\n`https://<site-name>.netlify.app` (fill in) · 10 test accounts, shared password from Netlify settings.\n\nWhat works today:\n- **Rules engine** for CFRA, PDL, FMLA, SDI, PFL, paid sick leave, bereavement and reproductive loss, checked against sources in October 2026.\n- **Deadline clock**: 5 business days for the first notice, 15 calendar days for certification, 5 business days to designate. It skips weekends and company holidays.\n- **Server-side privacy**: managers never receive the reason for a leave, and employees only receive their own records.\n- **Manager flag**: when a manager hears about a possible leave, the notice clock starts.\n\n## AI Value Archetype\n\n**Copilot** (proposed; needs your confirmation).\n\nThe prototype is a deterministic rules engine with no AI in it yet. The AI layer the bet depends on sits on top of it, and the rules engine stays the only thing that decides eligibility:\n\n1. **Intake.** An employee or manager writes in plain words (\"my dad has surgery on the 12th, I need a few weeks\"). The copilot turns that into a draft case: likely leave type, family relation, dates. HR confirms it with one click.\n2. **Plain-language answers.** Employees ask \"will I get paid?\" and get an answer grounded in their own case, in English or Spanish.\n3. **Notice drafting.** The copilot fills in the eligibility, designation and return-to-work notices from the case for HR to review and send.\n\nThe copilot never decides eligibility or denies a leave; a human signs off on every notice. That boundary is the trust story for Module 4.\n\nWhy not the other archetypes: an **Automator** that sends notices on its own is too risky for legal notices. An **Oracle** with no workflow is what ChatGPT already gives away for free.\n\n## The Bet in One Sentence\n\nCalifornia employers with 5 to 200 employees get every leave right the first time: Leave Ledger turns \"I need time off\" into the right state and federal leaves, pay and deadlines, so a part-time office manager does not need an employment lawyer or a leave vendor built for 5,000-person companies.\n\n**Why now:**\n- CFRA reached employers with 5+ employees in 2021.\n- California keeps adding leave rules: bereavement (2023), reproductive loss and 40 hours of sick leave (2024), and PFL for a designated individual from July 2028.\n- The AI-native leave tools that launched in 2026 (Aidora, now part of Paylocity; Mitratech Leave) are built for mid-market and enterprise.\n\n## Kill Criteria\n\nStop, or pivot to an add-on inside a payroll platform, if any of these happen:\n\n| # | Evidence that kills or changes the bet | Check by |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Fewer than 3 of 10 interviewed California office managers or HR generalists handled a CFRA or PDL leave in the last 12 months and called it painful | Nov 23, 2026 |\n| 2 | Fewer than 2 of those 10 would pay at least $4 per employee per month (Tilt's lowest tier) or a flat monthly price of similar size | Nov 23, 2026 |\n| 3 | Gusto or Rippling ships California-specific leave stacking with a notice clock before Leave Ledger has 5 paying employers | Ongoing; review monthly |\n| 4 | AI intake classifies under 90% of a 50-case golden set into the right leave type | Built and tested in Module 4 |\n\nIf #4 fails, the bet survives as a rules-and-workflow product with no AI in the decision path, and the archetype is revisited.\n","02-the-moat/data-flywheel.md":"# Data Flywheel Map\n\n> Score each loop 1-5. Your weakest loop is where competitors attack first.\n> The four loops below are the M2 starting point - adapt if your product has 2 or 6 loops instead of 4.\n\n## Flywheel Loops\n\n| Loop | What It Measures | Score 1 | Score 5 | Score |\n|------|------------------|---------|---------|-------|\n| **Correction** | Do users fix AI outputs? Is that signal captured and reused? | No capture | Automated retraining | __/5 |\n| **Preference** | Does the product learn individual / team preferences over time? | Stateless | Deep personalization | __/5 |\n| **Domain Context** | Does usage in one area improve quality in adjacent areas? | Siloed | Cross-domain transfer | __/5 |\n| **Network** | Does each new user / team make the product better for everyone? | Isolated | Strong network effects | __/5 |\n\n### Correction Loop - __/5\n**What you capture today:**\n**How it compounds:**\n\n### Preference Loop - __/5\n**What you capture today:**\n**How it compounds:**\n\n### Domain Context Loop - __/5\n**What you capture today:**\n**How it compounds:**\n\n### Network Loop - __/5\n**What you capture today:**\n**How it compounds:**\n\n**Total Flywheel Score: __/20**\n**Weakest Loop:**\n**Fix for weakest loop:**\n\n---\n\n## Encroachment Threat Assessment\n\n### 1. Platform Encroachment\n**Attacker:**\n**Vector:**\n**Time-to-threat:**\n**% of value at risk:**\n\n### 2. Vertical Competitor\n**Attacker:**\n**Vector:**\n**Time-to-threat:**\n**% of value at risk:**\n\n### 3. Adjacent Expansion\n**Attacker:**\n**Vector:**\n**Time-to-threat:**\n**% of value at risk:**\n\n---\n\n## 90-Day Encroachment Plan\n\n*Your partner played the Big Tech attacker. What was their plan to kill you?*\n\n**Attacker:**\n**Attack vector (target the weakest loop):**\n**Weeks 1-4 - what they ship:**\n**Weeks 5-8 - how they poach users:**\n**Weeks 9-12 - why users don't come back:**\n**Your defense:**\n","02-the-moat/kill-switch.md":"# Kill Switch Audit\n\n## Vendor Dependency Assessment\n\n| Dimension | Current State | Risk Level | 48-Hour Action |\n|-----------|--------------|------------|---------------|\n| **Provider** | | H / M / L | |\n| **Abstraction** | | H / M / L | |\n| **Routing** | | H / M / L | |\n| **Eval** | | H / M / L | |\n\n## Portability Score\n<!-- Ready / Partial / Locked -->\n\n## If [primary vendor] doubles pricing tomorrow:\n<!-- What's your 48-hour response? -->\n\n## If [primary vendor] ships a competing product:\n<!-- What's defensible that they can't replicate? -->\n","03-the-margin/cost-curve.md":"# Cost Curve & Pricing Strategy\n\n## Cost Model\n\n| Cost Category | Per-User/Month | Notes |\n|--------------|----------------|-------|\n| Inference (primary model) | | |\n| Inference (cascading/triage) | | |\n| Infrastructure | | |\n| Data/storage | | |\n| Human-in-the-loop | | |\n| **Total AI COGS** | | |\n\n## Cascading Strategy\n<!-- Cheap model → frontier model routing logic -->\n\n**Triage model:**\n**Frontier model:**\n**Routing rule:**\n**Expected cascade ratio:**\n\n## Pricing Model\n\n**Current pricing:**\n**Proposed AI pricing:**\n**Model:** seat-based / usage-based / outcome-based / hybrid\n\n## Stress Tests\n\n| Scenario | Impact on Margin | Response |\n|----------|-----------------|----------|\n| Inference costs 3x | | |\n| Heaviest segment doubles | | |\n| Model provider raises prices 50% | | |\n\n## Board One-Pager\n<!-- Before/After: Old SaaS revenue vs. AI usage revenue for your product -->\n\n**Before (traditional SaaS):**\n**After (AI-enabled):**\n**Net margin shift:**\n","04-the-contract/golden-dataset.md":"# Golden Dataset & Reliability Contract\n\n## Golden Dataset Spec\n\n| # | Input | Expected Output | Edge Case? | Judge Type |\n|---|-------|----------------|-----------|-----------|\n| 1 | | | Y/N | rule / LLM |\n| 2 | | | Y/N | rule / LLM |\n| 3 | | | Y/N | rule / LLM |\n| 4 | | | Y/N | rule / LLM |\n| 5 | | | Y/N | rule / LLM |\n\n**Adversarial rows included:** __\n**Coverage gaps identified by partner:**\n\n## Confidence UX Design\n\n**Approach:** show uncertainty / tiered confidence / human-in-loop trigger\n\n**High confidence (>90%):**\n**Medium confidence (70-90%):**\n**Low confidence (<70%):**\n\n**User control surface:**\n\n## Reliability Contract\n\n| Metric | Target | Measurement | Alert Threshold |\n|--------|--------|-------------|-----------------|\n| Accuracy | | | |\n| Hallucination rate | | | |\n| Latency (p95) | | | |\n| Drift velocity | | | |\n\n## HITL Architecture\n<!-- When does a human step in? What's the escalation path? -->\n\n## Red-Team Findings\n*What failure mode did your partner find that you missed?*\n","05-the-guardrails/compounding-system.md":"# Compounding System Design\n\n## Feedback Loops\n\n| Loop | Input | Output | Compounds? | Status |\n|------|-------|--------|-----------|--------|\n| | | | Y/N | active / broken / missing |\n| | | | Y/N | active / broken / missing |\n| | | | Y/N | active / broken / missing |\n\n**Broken loop identified by partner:**\n**Fix plan:**\n\n## Context Connectivity\n<!-- How does knowledge flow across teams and domains? Where does it silo? -->\n\n## Governance Policy\n\n**Scope:**\n**Autonomy boundaries:**\n**Escalation triggers:**\n**Audit cadence:**\n**Regulatory exposure (EU AI Act / other):**\n\n## Agent Topology\n<!-- If using agents: what can each agent do? What can't it do? Who approves what? -->\n\n## Shadow AI Audit\n\n| Tool | Owner | Risk Level | Decision |\n|------|-------|-----------|----------|\n| | | H / M / L | keep / govern / kill |\n| | | H / M / L | keep / govern / kill |\n| | | H / M / L | keep / govern / kill |\n\n**Total tools found:**\n**Tools after triage:**\n**Estimated hidden spend:**\n","06-the-pitch/roadmap.md":"# Three-Horizon Roadmap & Board Pitch\n\n## Roadmap\n\n### Horizon 1 · Now (0-3 months)\n*Quick wins. Ship with existing capabilities.*\n\n| Initiative | Metric | Confidence |\n|-----------|--------|-----------|\n| | | H / M / L |\n| | | H / M / L |\n\n### Horizon 2 · Next (3-9 months)\n*Bets. Requires new capabilities or integrations.*\n\n| Initiative | Metric | Confidence |\n|-----------|--------|-----------|\n| | | H / M / L |\n| | | H / M / L |\n\n### Horizon 3 · Bet (9-18 months)\n*Moonshots. High uncertainty, high potential.*\n\n| Initiative | Metric | Confidence |\n|-----------|--------|-----------|\n| | | H / M / L |\n\n## Board Pitch\n\n**Thesis (1 sentence):**\n\n**The case:**\n1. Why now:\n2. What's defensible:\n3. The economics:\n\n**The risks:**\n1. Trust / failure modes:\n2. Scale / governance:\n3. Competitive:\n\n**The ask:**\n\n## M1 Baseline vs. Now\n*Your 3-sentence AI strategy from Module 1 vs. what you'd say now:*\n\n**M1 baseline:**\n\n**Now:**\n","README.md":"# Leave Ledger · AI Product Strategy\n\n> A living strategy built across 6 weekly sessions, using the Product School AI product strategy template. Each week adds one module. By week 6, this folder is the strategy: version-controlled and ready for a board.\n\n**The bet:** California leave of absence management for employers with 5 to 200 employees.\n**Last updated:** Oct 27, 2026 (Week 1)\n**Customer interviews:** 0 of 10 (kill criteria check on Nov 23, 2026)\n**Partner challenges done:** 0 of 6\n\n---\n\n## Strategy at a Glance\n\n| Component | Module | Week of | Status | Key Artifact |\n|-----------|--------|---------|--------|-------------|\n| **The Bet** | M1 | Oct 26 | [x] Draft, needs partner challenge | `01-the-bet/` |\n| **The Moat** | M2 | Nov 2 | [ ] | `02-the-moat/` |\n| **The Margin** | M3 | Nov 9 | [ ] | `03-the-margin/` |\n| **The Contract** | M4 | Nov 16 | [ ] | `04-the-contract/` |\n| **The Guardrails** | M5 | Nov 23 | [ ] | `05-the-guardrails/` |\n| **The Pitch** | M6 | Nov 30 | [ ] | `06-the-pitch/` |\n\n---\n\n## The Bet (M1)\n\n**What we're building, for whom, why now.**\n\n- **Product:** Leave Ledger. Works out which California and federal leaves an employee qualifies for, what the state pays, and the notices and deadlines the employer owes. Separate views for HR, employee and manager.\n- **AI Value Archetype:** Copilot (confirmed Oct 26). AI handles intake, plain-language answers and notice drafts; a deterministic rules engine decides eligibility; a human signs off on every notice.\n- **Vulnerability Scores:** Moat 2/5 · Data 1/5 · Platform 4/5 (exposed)\n- **Top Risk:** Distribution. Small businesses buy HR through their payroll provider. If Gusto or Rippling ships California leave first, a standalone tool has no way in.\n- **Confidence:** L (until 10 customer interviews)\n- **Prototype:** Netlify test site, 10 test accounts (link in `01-the-bet/prototype.md`)\n- **Kill Criteria:** Under 3 of 10 interviewees feel the pain, or under 2 of 10 would pay $4 per employee per month, by Nov 23; or a payroll platform ships California leave stacking before we have 5 paying employers.\n\n→ Details: [`01-the-bet/`](01-the-bet/)\n\n---\n\n## The Moat (M2) · Week of Nov 2\n\n**Why this won't get copied in 6 months.**\n\n- **Data Flywheel Score:** __/20\n- **Weakest Loop:**\n- **Competitive Position:** [describe axes + placement]\n- **Encroachment Defense:**\n- **Vendor Portability:** Ready / Partial / Locked\n\n→ Details: [`02-the-moat/`](02-the-moat/)\n\n---\n\n## The Margin (M3) · Week of Nov 9\n\n**Will this make money or bleed it?**\n\n- **Gross Margin (current):**\n- **Gross Margin (AI-adjusted):**\n- **Pricing Model:**\n- **Cascading Strategy:**\n- **Break-even at:**\n\n→ Details: [`03-the-margin/`](03-the-margin/)\n\n---\n\n## The Contract (M4) · Week of Nov 16\n\n**Why users will trust a probabilistic system.**\n\n- **Reliability Target:**\n- **Golden Dataset:** __ rows, __ adversarial\n- **Confidence UX:** [approach]\n- **HITL Architecture:**\n- **Failure Mode Coverage:**\n\n→ Details: [`04-the-contract/`](04-the-contract/)\n\n---\n\n## The Guardrails (M5) · Week of Nov 23\n\n**What breaks when this scales, and what compounds.**\n\n- **Compounding System:** [describe feedback loops]\n- **Governance Posture:** [approach]\n- **Shadow AI Status:** __ tools found, __ triaged\n- **Agent Boundaries:**\n- **Regulatory Exposure:**\n\n→ Details: [`05-the-guardrails/`](05-the-guardrails/)\n\n---\n\n## The Pitch (M6) · Week of Nov 30\n\n**How you get this funded, shipped, and adopted.**\n\n- **Horizon 1 (Now):**\n- **Horizon 2 (Next):**\n- **Horizon 3 (Bet):**\n- **Board Narrative:** [1-sentence thesis]\n- **Key Metric:**\n\n→ Details: [`06-the-pitch/`](06-the-pitch/)\n\n---\n\n## Open decisions (Week 1)\n\n1. ~~Confirm the archetype.~~ Done Oct 26: Copilot, with the rules engine deciding eligibility.\n2. **Partner challenge.** Test the three named attackers (Tilt, ChatGPT, Gusto) and the 1/5 data score with your course partner. Record what they push on in `01-the-bet/diagnostic.md`.\n3. **Prototype link.** Add the Netlify URL to `01-the-bet/prototype.md`.\n4. **Start interviews.** Kill criteria 1 and 2 need 10 conversations by Nov 23, so about 3 a week. Update the interview count at the top of this file as you go.\n\n## Changelog\n\n| Week | Date | What changed |\n|------|------|-------------|\n| 1 | Oct 26, 2026 | M1 drafted: market sizing, three-axis diagnostic, prototype bet, kill criteria |\n| 1 | Oct 27, 2026 | Copilot archetype confirmed; interview and partner challenge counters added for the progress page |\n"}};
